# HAND COPY of the router-pair group in prometheusrule.yaml (this dir) — the drift pin in
# scripts/prometheus-rules-lint.sh asserts expr+for equality per alert name against the CR.
# Re-render with:  devbox run -- yq '.spec.groups[] | select(.name == "router-pair")' prometheusrule.yaml
# Loaded by router-pair.promtool-test.
groups:
  - name: router-pair
    # The CARP router pair (ADR-144, docs/router-move.md — **WAN follows the master**). Seen from
    # the HYPERVISORS, not the guests: each node's WAN gate (router-wangate@<vmid>, ansible/
    # pve-router-killswitch.yml) watches its VM's LAN tap for CARP adverts — only a MASTER sends
    # them — and writes router_node_carp_master / router_node_wan_link / a heartbeat to this
    # host's textfile. The gate is also what holds a non-master's WAN link down, so a master
    # COUNT != 1 is the pair's defining failure: 2 = split-brain (both answer the VIPs, and with
    # the gate both WANs are up wearing the ONE leased MAC), 0 = nobody routes. A failover passes
    # through 0 for ~4 s (3 x advbase + the gate's hold) — `for: 1m` rides that out.
    # The pair is exactly two nodes: fewer than two heartbeats is a gate (or a node's
    # hypervisor) gone dark, which leaves the count unreadable.
    rules:
      - alert: RouterPairMasterCount
        expr: sum(router_node_carp_master{job="pve-node"}) != 1
        for: 1m
        labels: {severity: critical, triage: none}
        annotations:
          summary: "Router pair has {{ $value }} CARP masters (want exactly 1)"
          description: >-
            The router nodes' WAN gates see {{ $value }} VMs advertising CARP. 2 = SPLIT-BRAIN: the nodes cannot hear each other's adverts (the LAN between them — vmbr0, the switch, multicast/IGMP snooping — or a pf rule), both hold every CARP VIP and both have their WAN up with the one leased MAC. 0 = no router is master: nothing answers the VIPs. Per node: `bash scripts/opnsense-router-node.sh check nx02|pve` (the CARP and WAN-gate lines), `router_node_carp_master` by host/vmid, and the gate log /var/log/router-killswitch/<vmid>-wangate.log on the hypervisor. Split-brain fix order: find the advert path (tcpdump `ip proto 112` on each hypervisor's vmbr0), then put one node in CARP maintenance.
      - alert: RouterWanGateSilent
        expr: >-
          (time() - max by (host, vmid) (router_node_wangate_heartbeat_seconds{job="pve-node"})) > 120 or on() (count(router_node_wangate_heartbeat_seconds{job="pve-node"}) < 2) or on() absent(router_node_wangate_heartbeat_seconds{job="pve-node"})
        for: 5m
        labels: {severity: warning, triage: none}
        annotations:
          summary: "Router WAN gate silent{{ with $labels.host }} on {{ . }}{{ end }} — the pair's master count is unreadable"
          description: >-
            A router node's WAN gate has not written its heartbeat for 2+ minutes, or fewer than the pair's two gates report at all. The gate is what holds a non-master's WAN down and what RouterPairMasterCount reads, so both are blind. On the hypervisor: `systemctl status router-wangate@<vmid>` and the gate log in /var/log/router-killswitch/; node_exporter's textfile dir /var/lib/prometheus/node-exporter/router-wangate-*.prom. Re-run ansible/pve-router-killswitch.yml to reassert the units.
